   "format": "short_answer",
   "stem": {
      "text": "The function f is defined by f(x) = (x^2 - 1) / (x + 2) for x < 1 and f(x) = sqrt(x - 1) / (x - 5) for x >= 1. State the intervals on which f is continuous, justify continuity at x = 1, and give the number of separate maximal intervals."
   },
   "answer_key": {
      "form": "numeric",
      "mathjson": 3
   },
   "worked_solution": [
      {
         "step": 1,
         "text": "On x < 1 the first rule is undefined only at x = -2. On x >= 1 the radicand is nonnegative and the second rule is undefined only at x = 5.",
         "mathjson": [
            "Add",
            "x",
            2
         ]
      },
      {
         "step": 2,
         "text": "Each rule is continuous on its domain, the first being rational and the second a quotient of a square root and a polynomial. At the join the left limit is (1 - 1) / 3 = 0 and f(1) = 0 / (-4) = 0, so f is continuous at x = 1.",
         "mathjson": 0
      },
      {
         "step": 3,
         "text": "f is continuous on (-infinity, -2), (-2, 5) and (5, infinity).",
         "mathjson": 5
      },
      {
         "step": 4,
         "text": "Those are three separate maximal intervals.",
         "mathjson": 3
      }
   ],
